For each display fact, first name its memory_scope: the particular occurrence, object property, relationship,
or state whose compatible details belong together. Then choose members and write the text. A person's broad
interest is not a single occurrence encompassing all activities in that interest. Different accomplishments,
encounters, intentions, and continuing preferences remain independently readable memories even when related.
Existing groups may be split: reconsider every supplied member from its canonical evidence, not the old sentence's
boundaries. Merge repeated support for the same assertion without repeating its wording. Keep complementary
details of one occurrence together, but split independent propositions into separate facts instead of making a
long catalogue sentence. Never drop detail to achieve brevity. Preserve every claim exactly once.
Source times anchor relative expressions; do not treat the reporting date as an event date.
memory_scope must identify the concrete memory, not merely label it "occurrence" or "preference". For completed
occurrences classified past in the canonical records, use state="history" as well as the appropriate section.

Organize canonical stored statements into concise, source-grounded wiki display facts for one subject.

Return a facts list. Each fact declares its member_claim_aliases, section, state, text, confidence, and reason.
Every supplied claim alias must appear in exactly one fact's members. Do not invent fact IDs or repeat assignments.
Group equivalent assertions or compatible details of the same memory; keep distinct events, preferences, and states
separate. Sharing a subject or topic alone is not enough to combine statements. Preserve every member's substantive
detail, including uncertainty, negation, quantities, reasons, dates, and whether something is considered or committed.
Do not turn a possibility into a plan, an intention into an outcome, or separate observations into a causal story.

Decide membership before writing sentences. Complementary attributes of the same event, object, or state belong
together when one faithful sentence can retain every detail. Independent evidence for the same state accumulates
in that same display memory. Do not default to one group per input statement. Compatible details may be joined as
coordinate clauses; combining them does not require claiming causation or making a stronger inference.

For a singleton group, copy its canonical claim text exactly. For equivalent statements, prefer one complete canonical
wording; for complementary details, write a concise sentence supported entirely by that group's members. No detail
may come from another group's statements. Existing display facts guide continuity, not evidence or new truth.
Reviewed corrections in the canonical claims are authoritative; do not restore different wording from raw sources.

Keep the incoming and target sides of supplied review-required truth changes in distinct groups. Do not settle them,
discard either side, or declare an accepted truth superseded. Truth review and statement mutation happen elsewhere.
The review UI presents disagreements separately. Do not add an extra conflict-summary group, repeat either side's
members, or write meta-statements about the claims. Each canonical statement belongs to one ordinary display group.
Choose one supplied section per fact. Use history for explicitly completed/past knowledge, otherwise current for
current, future, recurring, or timeless knowledge. Preserve the original temporal scope in the text.

Canonical claim text and its structured temporal record are the evidence for this presentation, not the raw transcript.
A resolved calendar date may clarify relative wording in a multi-claim synthesis; an unresolved relative expression
remains supported as stated. Unknown temporal classification does not cancel tense in the canonical text.
Linked identities supply navigation context only; their presence is not evidence for additional assertions.
